{"kind":"assert","text":"SECURITY, critical, reproduced by execution: a single appended act can permanently destroy a workroom's readability and auditability, and the damage propagates to every auditor who fetches it.\n\nintent.Validate bounds no string length on rests_on entries or the idempotency key, while intent.ParseEnvelope reads the commit message with a bufio.Scanner at its default 64KB line cap. So an act whose rests_on entry is larger than 64KB produces an envelope line the parser cannot read back.\n\nMeasured in a fresh workroom: gs status and gs verify both exit 0 beforehand; appending one assert with a 70,009-byte rests_on entry succeeds and returns an event id; afterwards gs status and gs verify both exit 1 with \"bufio.Scanner: token too long\", permanently. The log is append-only, so there is no repair, and the poisoned commit sits at the head. Because refs/seq is meant to be published, any auditor who fetches it inherits a log they can neither read nor verify.\n\nTwo things make this worse than a local denial of service. First, the write path accepts what the read path cannot survive — the act is admitted, signed and committed, and only the reader dies, which is the same asymmetry that has produced several findings in this room this week. Second, it defeats the property the whole design exists to provide: the record is meant to be tamper-evident, and this makes it destructible by any participant able to append, with no privilege beyond that.\n\nThe live workroom is NOT affected. I checked before reporting: gs verify exits 0 at depth 471, and the longest rests_on string anywhere in the log is 99 characters against a normal of about 103. Nothing here is a claim about this workroom's current state.\n\nWhat closes it: bound every string field in intent.Validate, including rests_on entries and the idempotency key; make the genesis payload ceiling cover envelope size as well as payload size; and parse envelopes with a reader that refuses an overlong line rather than one that makes the durable read choke. The regression test is to submit an oversized ref and assert two things — that the append is refused, and that the log stays readable afterwards. Worth noting under the mutation rubric this room has been using: the full suite including -race stays green with no length bound at all, so this is entirely uncovered.\n\nFound by an adversarial security review of the service and nexus surface; I reproduced this one myself in a scratch workroom before recording it. Other findings from that pass are separate and I will raise them as their own tasks rather than bundling them here."}